2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.004 5.979KB 16.14s
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] A few observations, ordered by impact.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] • Correctness*
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - `reduce()` is `async` but the call site does `let count = reduce(lines, file.name)` without
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] `await`. So `count` is a Promise, the log prints a Promise rather than a number, and any rejection
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] inside `reduce` bypasses the surrounding `try/catch` (it becomes an unhandled rejection instead).
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] Because the loop only awaits `readTextFile`, the reduce for file N can still be mutating
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] `sessionCount`, `models`, `vendors`, `minDate`, and `maxDate` while file N+1 is being
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] processed — a data race on shared state that would be avoided by awaiting.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - `const s3 = trim.indexOf("[roha] [FORGE]")` is tested with `s3 > 0`. A marker at offset 0 would
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] be missed; `>= 0` or `!== -1` matches the intent.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - `tag = trim.substring(s1 + 1, s2)` degenerates when the line has only one space: `s2` is `-1`,
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] and `substring` swaps/clamps its arguments, so `tag` becomes the text before the space
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] rather than between. Worth guarding.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - `parseInt(trim2.substring(0, s1), 16)` can be `NaN` on a malformed mark; `slopDate(NaN)` silently
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] produces `"Invalid Date"` in the history file and can also pollute the min/max bounds (depending on
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] comparison behaviour).
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - `minDate`/`maxDate` are initialised to `MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` and `0`; if no marks are parsed, the
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] summary line formats nonsense. A guard for "no marks seen" would be cleaner.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] • Data model / reporting*
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - `slopmark()` and `slopSeconds()` are dead — never called. `slopSeconds` also duplicates the
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] min/max bookkeeping already done in `slopDate`, so it's a trap for a future reader.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - `bigCount` is counted but never reported, and `m` (the model count in `Connected to <vendor> <m>
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] <s>`) is parsed and discarded. Either is probably intended to feed `models` or the vendor stats.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - The vendor cleanup `if (vendors[vendor].maxTime == 0) delete vendors[vendor]` removes
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] failure-only vendors. That may be deliberate, but it discards exactly the "endpoint failure" signal
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] you went to the trouble of parsing; if the goal is a usage report, reporting failures without
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] connections seems more useful than dropping them.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - `sessionCount` increments per header run (via `inHeader`), while `stats.sessions` only
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] increments when the model changes within a session. Worth a comment, since "sessions" means
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] something slightly different in the two places.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] • Robustness*
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - `Deno.writeTextFile("history/" + name, …)` assumes `history/` already exists. If it doesn't, the
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] failure is either swallowed by the `catch` (with the exception object discarded) or, given the
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] missing `await`, escapes entirely. Logging `e` would make the failure diagnosable.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - The `catch` in the loop reports only the filename; the actual error message is lost.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] • Style / structure*
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - `vendors` and `models` are inferred as `any`-ish empty objects. Explicit interfaces would make
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] the `?? { … }` defaults self-checking.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - `reduce` mixes line filtering, global aggregation, and filesystem I/O. Splitting "parse a line
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] into a record" from "write the reduced file" would make it testable and would let the aggregation
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] stay synchronous and race-free.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] - The commented-out `console.log` calls in `parseHeader` are the only trace of unrecognised header
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] lines; an explicit "unhandled header" counter might be more useful than toggling logs.
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] The 1/16 s (62.5 ms) tick encoding itself looks internally consistent between `slopmark`/`slopDate`
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] and the `+14` offset for `"[roha] [FORGE]"` is correct (6 + 1 + 7).
2026-10-01 18:52:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:54:10 [skid@archlinux] can i set a recent date to now - 120 days and use that as optional cutoff when refining
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 6.070KB 5.69s
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] Yes. Since every line's leading hex mark is just a count of 62.5 ms intervals from `epoch`, you can
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] convert a "now - N days" cutoff into the same units and compare before emitting the line. Make it
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] optional by only applying it when a positive day count is supplied (e.g. via `Deno.args`).
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] Sketch of the changes:
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] const cutoffDays=Deno.args.length>0?parseInt(Deno.args[0]):0;
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] const cutoffSixteenths=cutoffDays>0
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] 	?Math.floor((Date.now()-cutoffDays*86400000-epoch)/62.5)
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] 	:0;
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] Then inside `reduce`, after you compute `mark`:
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] const mark=parseInt(trim2.substring(0,s1),16);
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] if(cutoffSixteenths>0&&mark<cutoffSixteenths){
2026-10-01 18:54:17 [deepseek-flash] 	count++;
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] 	continue;
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] const date=slopDate(mark);
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] Notes:
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] - Place the check before `slopDate(mark)` so skipped lines don't influence `minDate`/`maxDate`.
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] - `count++` keeps the existing "skipped" accounting consistent; drop it if you'd rather those lines
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] be invisible in the summary.
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] - If you also want the header/vendor/model statistics to reflect only the retained window, move the
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] cutoff check above the `parseHeader` / tag handling block instead.
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] - Invoke with `deno run -A refine.ts 120` for a 120-day window, or with no arg for full history.
2026-10-01 18:54:18 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:16 [skid@archlinux] what is 8640000?????
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 7.524KB 5.82s
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] The number I used is 86,400,000 (`86400000`), not `8640000`.
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] - `86,400,000` = milliseconds in one day
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash]   `24  60  60 * 1000 = 86,400,000`
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] - `8,640,000` = `86,400,000 / 10`, which is 2.4 hours
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] So `cutoffDays * 8640000` would be 10× too small. For `cutoffDays = 120` you’d get 12 days instead
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] of 120.
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] To avoid counting zeros, define it as a named constant:
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] const MS_PER_DAY = 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] const cutoffSixteenths = cutoffDays > 0
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 	? Math.floor((Date.now() - cutoffDays * MS_PER_DAY - epoch) / 62.5)
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 	: 0;
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] Or with numeric separators, which TypeScript/Deno accept:
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] const MS_PER_DAY = 86_400_000;
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] That makes it clear it’s 86 million, not 8.6 million.
2026-10-01 18:55:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 14.13KB 8.30s
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] The cutoff works, but it sits a bit late in the loop. Two things to be aware of:
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] • 1. `slopSeconds` updates `minDate`/`maxDate` before the cutoff check*
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] const secs=slopSeconds(mark);   // <- mutates min/max
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] if(secs<recent){
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 	continue;                    // too late, range already widened
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] const date=slopDate(mark);
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] So the final `"usage from X to Y"` line reports the range of the raw input, not of what
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] actually landed in `history/`. If you want the report to describe the refined window, compute the
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] seconds without touching min/max until after the check. That also removes the need for two
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] near-identical functions:
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] function slopTime(sixteenths:number):Date{
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 	return new Date(epoch+sixteenths*62.5);
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] // in the loop
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] const mark=parseInt(trim2.substring(0,s1),16);
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] const date=slopTime(mark);
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] if(date.getTime()<recent){
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 	continue;
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] minDate=Math.min(minDate,mark);
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] maxDate=Math.max(maxDate,mark);
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] result.push(format(date,"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss")+trim2.substring(s1));
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] • 2. Stats are also outside the window*
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] `sessionCount`, `failCount`, `vendors` and `models` are all populated before the cutoff check, so
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] the histograms at the end cover the full raw history rather than the last 120 days. If you want the
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] report to match the file, the cutoff has to be tested before the `[roha] [FORGE]` header parsing
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] and tag handling. That requires reading the mark first — the mark is at the start of every line, so
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] you can parse it up front and `continue` early. The header detection is positional though (it flips
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] `inHeader` based on seeing `[roha] [FORGE]`), so skipping a header line would also change how the
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] following lines are classified. Worth deciding whether you care about that coupling before
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] moving the check.
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] • Minor*
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] - `bigCount` and the local `m` in `parseHeader` are unused.
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] - `recent` reads like a date but is a millisecond timestamp; `recentMillis` or `cutoff` is clearer.
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] - If you still want it optional, gate it on `Deno.args` as you asked before:
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] const days=Deno.args.length>0?parseInt(Deno.args[0]):0;
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] const recent=days>0?Date.now()-days*dayMillis:0;
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] With `recent=0` nothing is filtered, and you can drop the `secs<recent` guard's edge case since
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] every mark is positive.
2026-10-01 18:57:40 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 22.32KB 4.22s
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] The units now line up — `slopSeconds` returns seconds since epoch and `1e3 *` brings it to
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] milliseconds to compare with `recent`. That part is correct.
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] The remaining issue is that `slopSeconds` has a side effect: it updates `minDate` and `maxDate`
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] before you decide to keep or drop the line. So the final
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] console.log("nitrologic slop fountain relay usage from",slopDate(minDate),"to",slopDate(maxDate));
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] still reports the range of the raw input, not of the lines actually written to `history/`.
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] Same for `sessionCount`, `failCount`, `vendors` and `models` — they're all populated before the
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] cutoff test.
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] If you want the summary to describe the refined window, split the conversion from the bookkeeping:
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] function slopTime(sixteenths:number):Date{
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 	return new Date(epoch+sixteenths*62.5);
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] // inside the loop
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] const mark=parseInt(trim2.substring(0,s1),16);
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] const date=slopTime(mark);
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] if(date.getTime()<recent){
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 	continue;
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] minDate=Math.min(minDate,mark);
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] maxDate=Math.max(maxDate,mark);
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] result.push(format(date,"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss")+trim2.substring(s1));
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] That also removes the double work in `slopSeconds`/`slopDate` and makes `slopDate(minDate)` at the
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] end side-effect free.
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] Two smaller things:
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] - `slopmark` is now unused.
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] - `bigCount` and `m` in `parseHeader` are unused.
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] Whether the stats should be filtered is a design choice — if you're using the history/ files as the
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] artifact, having the printed histograms describe a different window than the files is the kind of
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] mismatch that bites later.
2026-10-01 19:00:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 29.71KB 4.42s
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] The min/max bookkeeping is now on the right side of the cutoff — `slopSeconds` is pure and
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] `slopDate` is only called for lines that are actually kept. So the final `from … to …` line now
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] describes the refined window. That's the fix you wanted.
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] A few remaining odds and ends:
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] • Empty window*
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] If every line is filtered out, `minDate` stays at `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`. The final
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] `slopDate(minDate)` will then compute `epoch + MAX_SAFE_INTEGER * 62.5` — roughly year 4.4 billion
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] — and print something absurd. Guard it:
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] if(maxDate>0){
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 	console.log("nitrologic slop fountain relay usage from",slopDate(minDate),"to",slopDate(maxDate));
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] • Stats still cover the raw input*
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] `sessionCount`, `failCount`, `vendors` and `models` are all updated before the cutoff test, so the
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] printed histograms don't match the files in `history/`. If that's intentional (raw summary
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] alongside refined output), fine — but the log doesn't say so, and a future reader will assume they
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] agree.
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] • Unused*
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] - `slopmark` — nothing calls it now.
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] - `bigCount` — incremented, never read.
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] - `m` in `parseHeader` — parsed, never used.
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] • Naming*
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] `slopSeconds` returns milliseconds-since-epoch / 1000, and you multiply by `1e3` immediately after.
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] Could return millis directly and drop the multiply:
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] function slopMillis(sixteenths:number):number{
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 	return epoch+sixteenths*62.5;
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] Then `if(slopMillis(mark)<recent) continue;` and `slopDate` calls `slopMillis` too. One less place
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] to get the unit wrong.
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] • Optional cutoff*
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] You settled on a hard-coded 120 days. If you later want the no-arg case to mean "no filter", the
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] pattern is:
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] const days=Deno.args.length>0?parseInt(Deno.args[0]):0;
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] const recent=days>0?Date.now()-days*dayMillis:0;
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 19:01:28 [deepseek-flash] with `recent=0` meaning everything passes, since every mark is positive.
