2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] Interpretation:
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] - `type -a ls` shows an alias/function. If `ls` is a function, `unset -f ls`; if it is an alias,
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] `unalias ls`. Then test `command ls`.
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] - `command ls` works but `ls` does not: your shell rc has a bad alias/function, possibly recursive.
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] Check `~/.bashrc`, `~/.bash_aliases`, `~/.profile`.
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] - `/bin/ls` fails too: check environment and binary:
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash]   env | grep -E 'LD_|LS_COLORS'
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash]   ldd /bin/ls
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash]   Bad `LD_PRELOAD` can make every dynamically linked program fail. `env -i /bin/ls` bypasses that.
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] - It hangs in a directory: likely stale NFS/CIFS or a huge directory. Check `mount`, `df -h .`, and
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] try:
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash]   timeout 5 ls -U
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] - It segfaults or gets killed: check kernel messages:
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash]   dmesg | tail -n 30
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash]   Exit 139 = segfault, 137 = SIGKILL/OOM. Reinstalling `coreutils` may fix a corrupted `ls`.
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] If the whole shell exits when you run `ls`, the cause is almost certainly an alias/function named
2026-10-01 22:19:17 [deepseek-flash] `ls` in your shell startup files. Run `type -a ls` from a clean shell or check those files.
