2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] Yes — `ls -d */` is the standard way to list only directories in the current directory.
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] How it works:
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] - `*/` is expanded by the shell, not by `ls`. It matches only directory entries.
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] - `-d` tells `ls` to show the directory names themselves, not their contents.
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] Caveats:
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] - Hidden directories are not included.
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] - If there are no matching directories:
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash]   - Bash by default passes the literal `/` to `ls`, causing an error like `ls: cannot access
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] '/': No such file or directory`.
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash]   - Zsh errors with `no matches found`.
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] - Directory names beginning with `-` can be mistaken for options. Using `--` helps:
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] ls -d -- */
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] If you want hidden directories too, or a more robust listing, `find` is usually better:
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] find . -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -type d
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] For just names, sorted:
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] find . -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -type d -printf '%f\n' | sort
2026-10-01 22:19:52 [deepseek-flash] 
