2026-10-01 22:27:30 [skid@archlinux] i want to check nvidia drivers are running corrctly
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 17.87KB 4.47s
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] Quick checks, in order:
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] • 1. Kernel driver loaded:*
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] lsmod | grep -E '^nvidia'
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] You should see `nvidia`, and usually `nvidia_modeset`, `nvidia_uvm`, `nvidia_drm`. `nvidia_uvm`
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] matters for CUDA workloads.
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] • 2. Driver version and GPU state:*
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] nvidia-smi
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] Expected: a table with GPU name, driver version, CUDA version, temperature, memory usage. If this
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] errors with “couldn’t communicate with the NVIDIA driver”, the module isn’t loaded or is a version
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] mismatch.
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] • 3. Kernel messages for the driver:*
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] dmesg | grep -i nvidia
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] Look for errors about version mismatch, missing firmware, or failed to initialize.
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] • 4. Which packages are installed:*
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] pacman -Qs nvidia
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] On Arch you want either `nvidia` (proprietary, current) or `nvidia-open` (open kernel modules) plus
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] `nvidia-utils`, `nvidia-settings`, and `lib32-nvidia-utils` if you run 32-bit apps. The running
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] module version must match the installed package:
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] cat /proc/driver/nvidia/version
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] pacman -Q nvidia-utils
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] If those disagree, you upgraded the package without rebooting.
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] • 5. CUDA toolkit (optional, only if you compile CUDA):*
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] pacman -Qs cuda
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] nvcc --version
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] You don’t strictly need the CUDA toolkit for PyTorch — the wheel ships its own runtime. But
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] `nvidia-smi` reporting a CUDA version is the max supported by the driver, not what’s installed.
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] • 6. PyTorch seeing the GPU, inside env3:*
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] source env3/bin/activate
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] python -c "import torch; print(torch.__version__); print(torch.version.cuda); print(torch.cuda.is_available()); print(torch.cuda.get_device_name(0) if torch.cuda.is_available() else 'no gpu')"
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] Expected: `is_available()` is `True` and the device name matches your card. If `torch.version.cuda`
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] is `None`, you installed a CPU-only wheel and need to reinstall with the CUDA index URL.
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] • 7. Ultralytics actually using the GPU:*
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] python -c "from ultralytics import YOLO; m = YOLO('models/yolov8n.pt'); m.predict('data/images/84802.png', device=0, verbose=True)"
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] Watch for the device line in the output — it should say `device=0` (or `cuda:0`), not `cpu`.
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] • Common failure modes on Arch:*
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] - Driver/package version skew after an upgrade → reboot, or downgrade `nvidia-utils` to
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] match the loaded module.
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] - `nvidia` module fails to load → often a kernel mismatch. Check `uname -r` against `pacman
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] -Q linux` and whether you have `linux-headers` installed for DKMS builds (`nvidia-dkms`).
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] - Secure Boot blocking unsigned modules → `mokutil --sb-state`, sign the module or disable
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] Secure Boot.
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] - Wayland/compositor issues are separate from CUDA — `nvidia-smi` and PyTorch can work fine
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] even if the desktop is glitchy.
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] Paste the output of `nvidia-smi`, `lsmod | grep nvidia`, and the PyTorch one-liner if anything
2026-10-01 22:27:35 [deepseek-flash] looks off and I can narrow it down.
