2026-10-01 22:27:48 [skid@archlinux] in regards to my onnx scripts
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 20.99KB 4.69s
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] For your setup the relevant question is two-fold: does PyTorch see CUDA (for `.pt` models), and
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] does ONNX Runtime use the CUDA execution provider (for `.onnx` models). They’re independent.
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] • 1. PyTorch / Ultralytics path (`.pt` files like `yolov8n.pt`, `yolo11m-obb.pt`):*
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] cd ~/nitrologic/onnx
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] source env3/bin/activate
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] python -c "import torch; print(torch.__version__, torch.version.cuda, torch.cuda.is_available(), torch.cuda.get_device_name(0))"
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] Want: a CUDA version (e.g. `12.4`), `True`, and your GPU name. If `torch.version.cuda` is `None`,
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] that’s a CPU-only wheel.
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] Then confirm Ultralytics is picking the GPU:
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] yolo predict model=models/yolov8n.pt source=data/images/84802.png device=0 verbose=True
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] Look for `device=0` or `cuda:0` in the header, not `cpu`.
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] • 2. ONNX Runtime path (`.onnx` files like `yoloe_model.onnx`):*
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] python -c "import onnxruntime as ort; print(ort.__version__); print(ort.get_available_providers())"
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] You want `CUDAExecutionProvider` in that list. If you only see `CPUExecutionProvider`, you have
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] `onnxruntime` (CPU) installed instead of `onnxruntime-gpu`:
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] pip uninstall -y onnxruntime onnxruntime-gpu
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] pip install onnxruntime-gpu
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] Check the CUDA/cuDNN libs it can find:
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] python - <<'EOF'
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] import onnxruntime as ort
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] print(ort.get_device())
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] print(ort.get_available_providers())
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] EOF
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] If `CUDAExecutionProvider` is listed but inference still runs on CPU, ONNX Runtime couldn’t load
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] `libcublas` / `libcudnn` at runtime. The `onnxruntime-gpu` wheel bundles compatible CUDA libs for
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] recent versions; mismatched versions between torch’s CUDA and ORT’s expected CUDA is the usual
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] culprit.
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] • 3. In your scripts, check how the device/provider is chosen.*
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] Look at `predict.py` and `count.py`:
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] grep -nE 'device|providers|CUDAExecutionProvider|torch\.cuda|onnxruntime|SessionOptions' predict.py count.py
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] Typical patterns you want to see:
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] - Ultralytics: `model.predict(..., device=0)` or `model.to('cuda')`.
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] - Raw ONNX Runtime: `ort.InferenceSession(path, providers=['CUDAExecutionProvider',
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 'CPUExecutionProvider'])`. Provider order matters — CUDA first.
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] If the script hardcodes `providers=['CPUExecutionProvider']` or passes `device='cpu'`, that’s why
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] it’s slow, not a driver problem.
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] • 4. Sanity check that the driver itself is fine:*
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] nvidia-smi
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] If this works, the kernel driver is loaded and CUDA libraries from the wheels should be able to
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] talk to it. If it doesn’t, nothing above will help — fix the driver first.
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] • 5. If you want the actual device being used at runtime*, add this to whichever script is
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] the entry point and run it once:
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] import torch, onnxruntime as ort
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] print("torch cuda:", torch.cuda.is_available(), torch.cuda.get_device_name(0) if torch.cuda.is_available() else "")
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] print("ort providers:", ort.get_available_providers())
2026-10-01 22:27:53 [deepseek-flash] 
