2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 515B 9.65s
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] It depends what you mean by “simplest”:
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] - Just play files, minimal player: `mpg123`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Install: `sudo pacman -S mpg123`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Play: `mpg123 song.mp3`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Play a folder: `mpg123 *.mp3`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Controls: `mpg123 -C *.mp3` then use `p` pause, `s` stop, `f`/`b` next/back.
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] - One player for most formats, still simple: `mpv`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Install: `sudo pacman -S mpv`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Audio only: `mpv --no-video song.flac`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Playlist: `mpv --no-video ~/Music/*.flac`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Keys: space pause, `>` next, `<` previous, `q` quit.
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] - Simple TUI with library/playlists: `cmus`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Install: `sudo pacman -S cmus`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Run: `cmus`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Press `5` for file browser, `a` to add, `Enter` to play.
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] - Alternative simple TUI: `moc`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Install: `sudo pacman -S moc`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash]   - Run: `mocp`
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] For the absolute simplest command-line player, use mpg123. If you want broad format support
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] with almost no setup, use mpv. If you want a proper music library interface, use
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] cmus or moc.
2026-10-03 11:39:01 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:04 [skid@archlinux] how to set href to current position in anchor
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 1.723KB 10.63s
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] “Current position” is ambiguous. In HTML, an anchor can link to:
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] - the current page URL
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] - the current scroll position (via an element ID)
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] - the current URL hash
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] You cannot link directly to arbitrary X/Y scroll coordinates with a plain `href`. You link to an
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] element `id`.
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 1. Link to the current page URL
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] <a id="currentPageLink" href="">Current page</a>
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] function setAnchorToCurrentPage(anchor) {
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	anchor.href = window.location.href;
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] An empty `href=""` already points to the current URL in most browsers.
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 2. Link to the current scroll position
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] Find the element at the current scroll position, make sure it has an `id`, then set the anchor’s
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] `href` to `#that-id`.
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] <a id="currentPositionLink" href="#">Link to current position</a>
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] function setAnchorToCurrentPosition(anchor) {
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	var x = window.scrollX + window.innerWidth / 2;
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	var y = window.scrollY + window.innerHeight / 2;
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	var element = document.elementFromPoint(x, y);
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	if (!element) {
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 		return;
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	if (!element.id) {
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 		element.id = 'current-position-' + Date.now();
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	anchor.href = '#' + element.id;
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] This uses the centre of the viewport. Change `x` and `y` if you want the top-left or another point.
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 3. Update the URL to the current position
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] If you want the browser address bar to reflect the current position, use `history.replaceState`
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] after setting an `id`:
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] function updateUrlToCurrentPosition() {
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	var element = document.elementFromPoint(
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 		window.scrollX + window.innerWidth / 2,
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 		window.scrollY + window.innerHeight / 2
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	);
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	if (!element) {
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 		return;
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	if (!element.id) {
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 		element.id = 'current-position-' + Date.now();
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 	history.replaceState(null, '', '#' + element.id);
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 4. If you meant “do nothing” on click
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] Avoid `href="javascript:void(0)"`. Use a button or `href="#"` with `event.preventDefault()` if you
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] must.
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] If you meant something else by “current position”, clarify whether it is a scroll position, URL
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] hash, or an element in a list.
2026-10-03 14:37:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:02 [skid@archlinux] cmake for linux i want to relax warnings
2026-10-05 10:09:14 [skid@archlinux]    SET(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS} -w -std=c++20")
2026-10-05 10:09:14 [skid@archlinux] /home/skid/nitrologic/pipeline/zlib/gzlib.c: In function ‘gz_error’:
2026-10-05 10:09:14 [skid@archlinux] /home/skid/nitrologic/pipeline/zlib/gzlib.c:579:20: warning: old-style function definition [-Wold-style-definition]
2026-10-05 10:09:14 [skid@archlinux]   579 | void ZLIB_INTERNAL gz_error(state, err, msg)
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 810B 2.62s
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] The issue is that `gzlib.c` is a C file, so it's compiled with `CMAKE_C_FLAGS`, not
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] `CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS`. Your `-w` never reaches the C compiler.
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] Set both, and prefer per-target settings so you don't silently suppress warnings across your whole
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] project:
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] # Global (blunt) approach - applies to all C and C++ targets
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] if(CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID MATCHES "GNU|Clang")
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 	set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS   "${CMAKE_C_FLAGS}   -w")
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 	set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS "${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS} -w -std=c++20")
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] endif()
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] Better: only silence the third-party zlib target, keeping warnings for your own code:
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] # Assume zlib is built as a target named "zlib" (or zlibstatic)
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] if(TARGET zlib)
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 	target_compile_options(zlib PRIVATE -w)
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] elseif(TARGET zlibstatic)
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 	target_compile_options(zlibstatic PRIVATE -w)
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] endif()
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] If you'd rather suppress only that specific warning instead of everything:
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] target_compile_options(zlib PRIVATE -Wno-old-style-definition)
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] Notes:
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] - `-w` in `CMAKE_C_FLAGS` will also affect any C code you write, so use the per-target form if you
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] can.
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] - `-std=c++20` belongs in `CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD`/`CXX_STANDARD` rather than raw flags, but leaving it
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] as-is is fine if the rest of your build expects it.
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] - If zlib is pulled in via `FetchContent`/`ExternalProject`, set the flags on the fetched target
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] after `FetchContent_MakeAvailable`, not before.
2026-10-05 10:09:17 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:07 [skid@archlinux] > /home/skid/nitrologic/pipeline/libjasper/base/jas_stream.c: In function ‘jas_stream_tmpfile’:
2026-10-05 10:23:07 [skid@archlinux] /home/skid/nitrologic/pipeline/libjasper/base/jas_stream.c:535:13: error: implicit declaration of function ‘unlink’ [-Wimplicit-function-declaration]
2026-10-05 10:23:07 [skid@archlinux]   535 |         if (unlink(obj->pathname)) {
2026-10-05 10:23:07 [skid@archlinux] Command not recognised home/skid/nitrologic/pipeline/libjasper/base/jas_stream.c:
2026-10-05 10:23:07 [skid@archlinux] > you ok bot?
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 2.785KB 2.82s
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] I'm fine, thanks — that one's not a warning to relax, it's a real missing include.
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] `unlink()` is declared in `<unistd.h>`. `jas_stream.c` doesn't include it, so under C99+ (where
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] implicit declarations are errors) the compiler rejects the call. Adding `-w` won't fix it, and you
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] shouldn't — you'd just be masking a genuine bug.
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] Fix: add the include near the top of `jas_stream.c`:
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] #include <unistd.h>
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] Guard it if you care about non-POSIX platforms (the file is already POSIX-oriented given it uses
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] `unlink`, `tmpfile`, etc., so a plain include is normally fine):
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] #if defined(JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H) || defined(__unix__) || defined(__APPLE__)
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] #include <unistd.h>
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] #endif
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] If you'd rather not touch upstream sources, force the include from CMake instead:
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] target_compile_options(jasper PRIVATE -include unistd.h)
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] That tells the compiler to pretend `unistd.h` was included at the top of every translation unit. It
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] works, but patching the one file is cleaner.
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] Also worth checking: if your build is passing `-std=c99`/`-std=c11` (strict, no GNU extensions),
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] some older libjasper code assumes GNU defaults. `-std=gnu11` is the usual fix for that class of
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] breakage — but the `unlink` error specifically is the missing header, not the standard.
2026-10-05 10:23:10 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:00 [skid@archlinux] in vscode how to locate .c file
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 4.297KB 2.64s
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] A few ways, depending on what you mean by "locate":
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] • Jump to a file by name (fuzzy search)*
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] - `Ctrl+P`, type part of the name, e.g. `jas_stream.c`, Enter. This is the fastest way and works
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] across the whole workspace.
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] • Find a file in the explorer tree*
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] - `Ctrl+Shift+E` opens the Explorer sidebar, then drill down to the path.
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] • Reveal the file you have open in the tree*
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] - `Ctrl+Shift+E`, then click the "Reveal Active File in Explorer" icon (or `Ctrl+K R` / right-click
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] the tab → Reveal in Explorer).
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] • From a compiler error / terminal output*
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] - In the integrated terminal, `Ctrl+Click` on a `path/file.c:535` reference to open it at that
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] line. If it doesn't link, check the path is relative to your workspace root.
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] - In the Problems panel (`Ctrl+Shift+M`), click the entry to jump to the file.
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] • From `#include` or a symbol*
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] - `F12` (Go to Definition) on an include or function name.
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] - `Ctrl+T` to search symbols across the workspace.
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] • If `Ctrl+P` doesn't show it*
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] - The file may be excluded. Check `files.exclude` / `search.exclude` in settings, and if the
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] directory is outside the workspace, add it with File → Add Folder to Workspace. Also check
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] `.gitignore`-based exclusions if `search.useIgnoreFiles` is on — `Ctrl+P` still shows ignored
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] files, but the search panel won't.
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] For your case (`/home/skid/nitrologic/pipeline/libjasper/base/jas_stream.c`), `Ctrl+P` and typing
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] `jas_stream` should do it, provided the workspace root is `/home/skid/nitrologic/pipeline` or
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] higher.
2026-10-05 10:24:04 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:07 [skid@archlinux] i need to declare [200~JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H in cmake
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 6.072KB 3.41s
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] Add it as a compile definition on the libjasper target. Two options depending on how the target is
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] defined.
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] • If it's a normal target:*
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] target_compile_definitions(jasper PRIVATE JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H=1)
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] • If you don't know the exact target name*, or want it to apply regardless:
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] # Global fallback - affects every target in the project
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] add_compile_definitions(JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H=1)
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] Or via the old flag-style, which also works:
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS "${CMAKE_C_FLAGS} -DJAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H=1")
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] • What `PRIVATE` means here*
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] - `PRIVATE` — only libjasper's own sources see the define. This is what you want; it's an internal
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] config macro.
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] - `PUBLIC` — also propagates to anything linking libjasper. Unnecessary here.
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] - `INTERFACE` — only consumers, not libjasper itself. Not what you want.
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] • Watch out for a generated `config.h`*
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] Libjasper upstream is autotools-based and normally writes `JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H` into a generated
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] `jas_config.h` / `config.h`. If that header already exists and has:
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 10:25:11 [deepseek-flash] 
