2026-10-05 13:06:33 [skid@archlinux] what will be need, is it possible to generate a more useful api document for godot
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.015 1.377MB 23.75s
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] Yes, and it is a natural extension of what that script already does. The current output captures
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] shape (which classes exist and which category they fall into); it does not capture
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] interface. `ClassDB` exposes enough to close most of that gap.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] What ClassDB can give you
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] For each registered class:
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] - `class_get_method_list(class, no_inheritance)` — name, arguments (each with `name`, `type`,
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] `class_name`, `hint`, `hint_string`), return dict of the same shape, and `flags` you can mask
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] against `METHOD_FLAG_STATIC`, `METHOD_FLAG_VIRTUAL`, `METHOD_FLAG_VARARG`, `METHOD_FLAG_CONST`.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] - `class_get_property_list(class, no_inheritance)` — name, type, hint, `usage` flags, `class_name`.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] - `class_get_signal_list(class, no_inheritance)` — signal name and argument list.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] - `class_get_integer_constant_list` plus `class_get_integer_constant` and
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] `class_get_integer_constant_enum` — constant values and which enum they belong to.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] - `class_get_enum_list` and `class_get_enum_constants` — enum membership.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] - `get_parent_class`, `class_get_api_type` (`"core"`, `"editor"`, `"extension"`),
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] `can_instantiate`, `is_class_enabled`.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] Passing `no_inheritance = true` keeps each class entry compact and lets a consumer walk the parent
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] chain itself — which is usually what you want for tooling.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] What ClassDB cannot give you
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] Four things have to come from elsewhere:
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 1. Argument default values. The method dict does not carry them, so you cannot recover
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] `delta: float = 0.0` from reflection.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 2. Property setter/getter names. `PropertyInfo` holds them in C++ but they are not exported
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] into the scripting dictionary.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 3. Prose. Descriptions, notes, `[b]`-marked warnings, code samples, "see also".
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 4. `@GlobalScope` and `@GDScript` members. Global functions are not enumerable through
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] ClassDB; the current script hardcodes the two pseudo-class names for this reason.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] All four live in the class reference XML under `doc/classes/*.xml` in the Godot source tree, tagged
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] per release. Fetching the tag that matches `Engine.get_version_info().string` and parsing with the
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] built-in `XMLParser` is the straightforward path.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] Two realistic shapes
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] - Structural only. Everything ClassDB knows, no prose. Roughly 10–25 MB of JSON for a full
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 4.x tree, dropping to a couple of MB gzipped. Works at runtime, no network, no XML.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] - Merged with doc XML. Adds descriptions, defaults, setter/getter, globals. Multiplies the
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] raw size but makes the file self-sufficient offline.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] Either can be emitted as one file or split per class with a manifest — splitting is friendlier for
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] diffing between engine versions and for lazy loading.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] Caveats worth knowing up front
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] - Editor-only classes require an editor binary. A standalone `--script` run on a template build
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] will silently omit them; the current `editors.is_empty()` case would be the signal to watch.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] - `type_string` and `TYPE_MAX` are stable enough for the variant list, but the numeric range shifts
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] between releases.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] - The current `0.2.0` shape is a fine index. A richer document is a different artifact;
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] keeping both, with the index as a manifest pointing into per-class files, is probably the cleanest
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] arrangement.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] If you want, I can draft the extension to `scrapeSchema.gd` that adds methods, properties, signals,
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] constants, and enums per class — either as one expanded schema or as a manifest plus per-class
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] files. The doc-XML merge is a separate piece and can follow once the structural half is settled.
2026-10-05 13:06:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:58:57 [skid@archlinux] what is this path in arch
2026-10-05 13:58:58 [skid@archlinux] file:///run/user/1000/doc/ngHfyfxv_kKn8we9BMHIAQ/warren.png
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 4.147KB 7.13s
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] That path is from the XDG Desktop Portal document portal, not a normal directory on your
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] disk.
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] `/run/user/1000/doc/...` is a FUSE mount created by `xdg-document-portal`, part of
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] `xdg-desktop-portal`. It is used to give sandboxed applications (Flatpak, Snap, etc.)
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] access to files outside their sandbox without granting them full filesystem access.
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] - `/run/user/1000` is your user’s runtime directory (1000 is your UID).
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] - `/doc` is the document portal mount.
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] - `ngHfyfxv_kKn8we9BMHIAQ` is a random document ID generated for one specific file access.
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] - `warren.png` is the file that was shared with the sandboxed app.
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] So when a Flatpak app (like a browser, image viewer, or editor) opens a file via a file chooser,
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] the portal exposes that file at a path like this. The app can read it, but it cannot see the rest
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] of your home directory unless you grant it.
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] Key points:
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] - It is temporary: it disappears when the session ends or when the document is closed.
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] - It is not a copy: it is a live view of the original file via FUSE.
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] - It is safe: only your user and the sandboxed app can access it.
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] - The real file is wherever you originally opened it from (e.g., `~/Pictures/warren.png`).
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] If you want to find the original file while the path still exists, you can often use `lsof` on the
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] portal process or just copy the file from that path to a normal location.
2026-10-05 13:59:06 [deepseek-flash] 
