2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] Thanks — seeing it in context, the array is clean and functional, and the multi-user explanation
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] helps. Here's what I'd change and why, working through each line:
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] 
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] • Line 1 is the problem line. "Guest assistant language model" does double duty: it
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] sets tone ("mindful of others, courteous, professional" — sensible for multi-user) and it
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] implicitly defines an identity*. The tone part earns its keep; the identity part is what caused
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] the poisoning. Two ways to fix it:
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] 
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] - Strip the identity noun: "You are a shared assistant serving multiple users. Please be
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] mindful of others, courteous and professional." Now there's no name to contradict and no vacuum
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] inviting the model to reason about what it isn't.
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] - Or inject runtime identity if your templating layer can compose per-request: append
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] something like `"You are currently served as {model} via the {account} account."` This makes
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] self-reports accurate by construction, and users asking "which model is this" get a real answer.
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] Given your multi-account table, this is probably the better long-term fix — it turns the identity
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] question from a liability into a feature.
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] 
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] • Lines 2–3 are fine.* Clear, enforceable, no ambiguity.
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] 
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] • Line 4 has a parsing hazard: "over inlining code with arrow and map style
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] suggestions" is genuinely ambiguous — it can read as "prefer named functions over arrows" or*
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] as instructions about how suggestions should be delivered. A model skimming it could comply with
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] the wrong reading. Something like "When suggesting code, prefer named reusable functions over arrow
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] functions and inline map/lambda chains" removes the ambiguity.
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] 
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] • One addition worth considering*: an explicit identity fallback for the non-injected case
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] — "If asked which model or provider you are, state that this is determined by the harness and
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] cannot be answered from within the conversation." This prevents the model improvising an answer,
2026-10-23 19:41:31 [glm-5.3] which is exactly the failure you logged.
